# Target C-II — exact convex hull of physical pentagon turn vectors

**Date:** 2026-09-19  
**Claim class:** exact local convex-hull classification  
**Depends on:** `target_c_pentagon_three_gap_inequality_2026-09-19.md`; pentagon closure  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_pentagon_turn_convex_hull_2026_09_19.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

Let `S` be the set of normalized turn vectors

\[
x=(x_0,\ldots,x_4),
\qquad x_i=\delta_i/\pi,
\]

arising from strict closed equilateral physical force pentagons in the audited regime. Thus `sum x_i=2`. Define

\[
q_i=x_i+x_{i+1}-x_{i+3}.
\tag{1}
\]

The preceding three-gap theorem proves

\[
q_i>\frac13
\qquad\text{for every }i.
\tag{2}
\]

This note identifies the exact closed convex hull of `S`:

\[
\boxed{
\overline{\operatorname{conv}}(S)
=
\left\{x:\sum_i x_i=2,\ q_i\ge\frac13\text{ for all }i\right\}.
}
\tag{3}
\]

The right side is a four-simplex. Its five vertices are the cyclic rotations of

\[
\boxed{
\left(0,\frac23,\frac13,\frac13,\frac23\right).
}
\tag{4}
\]

Consequently the new three-gap inequalities are not just another collection of local linear bounds. Together with the angle sum they give the complete affine-linear information obtainable from the local physical-pentagon turn set. Any further exclusion of the surviving support-seven cases using local pentagon closure must use genuinely nonlinear information or information coupling different faces.

---

## 1. The `q` transform is unimodular

The linear map (1) has determinant one. Its inverse is

\[
\boxed{x_i=q_{i+1}-q_{i+2}+q_{i+3}.}
\tag{5}
\]

Also

\[
\sum_iq_i=\sum_ix_i=2.
\tag{6}
\]

The companion exact certificate verifies the determinant, inverse, and sum identity over the rationals.

Set

\[
t_i=q_i-\frac13.
\]

Then the closed inequalities `q_i>=1/3` become

\[
t_i\ge0,
\qquad
\sum_it_i=\frac13.
\tag{7}
\]

Thus the right side of (3), expressed in `q` coordinates, is literally the standard four-simplex. Its vertices have one `q` coordinate equal to `2/3` and the other four equal to `1/3`. Applying (5) gives exactly the five cyclic rotations of (4).

The three-gap theorem already gives

\[
S\subset\operatorname{int}P,
\]

where `P` denotes this closed simplex. Hence

\[
\overline{\operatorname{conv}}(S)\subseteq P.
\tag{8}
\]

It remains only to show that every vertex of `P` is a limit of strict physical pentagons.

---

## 2. An exact strict family approaching a simplex vertex

Take a small parameter

\[
0<\varepsilon<\frac\pi6
\]

and define

\[
\alpha(\varepsilon)
=
\arccos\left(\cos\varepsilon-\frac12\right).
\tag{9}
\]

For this interval,

\[
\frac\pi3<\alpha<\frac\pi2.
\]

Consider the five unit directions with arguments

\[
\varepsilon,
\quad
\pi-\alpha,
\quad
\pi,
\quad
\pi+\alpha,
\quad
2\pi-\varepsilon.
\tag{10}
\]

They are strictly ordered. Their vector sum is exactly zero:

\[
2\cos\varepsilon
-
\left(1+2\cos\alpha\right)
=0
\tag{11}
\]

by the definition of `alpha`. Therefore they are the side directions of a closed equilateral force pentagon.

Their positive cyclic gaps are

\[
\begin{aligned}
\delta_0&=\pi-\alpha-\varepsilon,\\
\delta_1&=\alpha,\\
\delta_2&=\alpha,\\
\delta_3&=\pi-\alpha-\varepsilon,\\
\delta_4&=2\varepsilon.
\end{aligned}
\tag{12}
\]

These satisfy all strict physical gap conditions. The only boundary-sensitive adjacent sum is

\[
\delta_3+\delta_4=\pi-\alpha+\varepsilon.
\]

It is strictly larger than `2pi/3` because

\[
\alpha<\frac\pi3+\varepsilon.
\tag{13}
\]

To verify (13), cosine is decreasing on the relevant interval and

\[
\begin{aligned}
\cos\alpha-
\cos\left(\frac\pi3+\varepsilon\right)
&=
\frac12\left(
\cos\varepsilon-1+\sqrt3\sin\varepsilon
\right)\\
&=
\sin\frac\varepsilon2
\left(
\sqrt3\cos\frac\varepsilon2-
\sin\frac\varepsilon2
\right)>0
\end{aligned}
\]

for `0<epsilon<pi/6`.

As `epsilon` tends to zero,

\[
\alpha(\varepsilon)\to\frac\pi3
\]

and the normalized gap vector (12) tends to

\[
\left(
\frac23,\frac13,\frac13,\frac23,0
\right),
\tag{14}
\]

which is one vertex of `P`. Cyclic relabeling gives strict families approaching all five vertices.

Therefore every vertex of `P` lies in `closure(S)`, so

\[
P=\operatorname{conv}(\text{vertices of }P)
\subseteq
\overline{\operatorname{conv}}(S).
\tag{15}
\]

Combining (8) and (15) proves (3).

---

## 3. The old linear turn bounds are consequences of the simplex

The exact certificate checks the five simplex vertices against the older closed inequalities

\[
0\le x_i\le\frac23,
\qquad
\frac23\le x_i+x_{i+1}\le1.
\tag{16}
\]

They hold at every vertex and therefore throughout `P` by convexity. In the interior `q_i>1/3`, all simplex barycentric weights are positive, and the physical strict versions are recovered.

Thus, after (2) is known, the older one-turn/two-turn linear inequalities do not cut the closed convex hull any further. They remain useful geometric statements, but they are not additional affine separation power.

---

## 4. Consequence for the support-seven strategy

The current twelve convex support-seven `H8` orbits survived the known affine triangle-angle equations and strict linear turn inequalities. Equation (3) explains why searching for another purely linear inequality in one physical pentagon cannot finish those cases: the local linear hull is now complete.

The next usable information must be nonlinear or inter-face. Two exact candidates already present in the repository are:

- the full equilateral-pentagon closure identities, such as the cosine equation used to prove the three-gap theorem;
- the eight support-seven `H8` sine-cycle integrability equations coupling radial ratios across the relation graph.

**Disposition:** the closed convex hull of strict physical pentagon turn vectors is exactly the four-simplex `sum x=2`, `x_i+x_{i+1}-x_{i+3}>=1/3`. No stronger affine-linear local pentagon inequality exists outside consequences of these five simplex facets.
